<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galerius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 258–<a href="/years/311">311</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galerius was a Roman emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a>, made Caesar under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> in 293 and senior emperor in the east from 305 to 311. He fought on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> against the Carpi and other peoples and won a major victory over the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narseh">Narseh</a> in 298. A leading force behind the Great Persecution of Christians, he ended it by an edict of toleration shortly before his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galerius.jpg" alt="Galerius" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shinjirod · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Romuliana_Galerius_head.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Galerius was of Dacian origin and was born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> provinces, either near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> or at the place where he later built a palace named after his mother, Felix Romuliana (Gamzigrad). His mother Romula had left Roman Dacia because of attacks by the Carpi. He followed his father's occupation as a herdsman and was nicknamed Armentarius, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for herd. He served with distinction under the emperors Aurelian and Probus, and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s daughter Valeria. In 293, at the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a>, he was named Caesar together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius Chlorus</a> and was entrusted with the Illyrian provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After campaigning against Sarmatians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, he took command on the eastern frontier. The Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narseh">Narseh</a> inflicted a severe defeat on him between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Carrhae</a> and Callinicum. Diocletian then made him walk a mile ahead of the imperial cart in his purple robes, which some scholars read as blame, while another sees ordinary procedure. In 298 Galerius reinforced his army and defeated Narseh in two battles, the second at Satala, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> seized Narseh's camp, treasury and wife. The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> of 299 made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> the boundary, returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> to Roman influence and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> control of five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a>. Galerius also defeated the Carpi in 297 and 300.</p><p>He was promoted to Augustus when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> abdicated in 305. After Constantius died in 306 Galerius reluctantly accepted Constantine as Caesar, and when Maxentius rebelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> he invaded but had to withdraw. At the conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a> in 308 it was agreed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> would be Augustus in the West. Seven claimants to power eventually existed, and Galerius spent his later years on public works such as a logging and drainage project at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-balaton">Lake Balaton</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Galerius was a fierce advocate of the old gods, and Christians credited the persecution beginning with the edict of 24 February 303 to his work. Some historians claim he provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> into it, although the fire in the palace he is said to have started is unproven. In April 311 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> he issued an edict of toleration, apparently during his last illness, and asked Christians to pray for him and the state. This ended official persecution, and Constantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> legalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> two years later in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan">Edict of Milan</a>.</p><p>He died in late April or early May 311 of a painful illness described by Eusebius and Lactantius. He was buried in a mausoleum at Gamzigrad-Romuliana, which was inscribed on the World Heritage List in June 2007. Lactantius, a Christian and adviser to Constantine, portrays him as hostile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, but Timothy Barnes and others suspect he tailored the facts to his thesis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Galerius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jews were at times exempt from official sacrifice, but Christians were persecuted: by Nero after the fire of 64 CE, under Decius’s edict of sacrifice, by Valerian, and by Diocletian’s edicts from 303, until <b>Galerius</b> revoked the policy in 311.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Several Roman and Byzantine emperors were wholly or partly of Thracian descent, among them Maximinus Thrax, <b>Galerius</b> and Leo I the Thracian, and Constantine the Great was born to a Thracian father of the Moesi tribe.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Gravely ill, Emperor <b>Galerius</b> granted Christians the right to worship, ending the Great Persecution in his territories.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-30">30 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Galerius</b> granted him only the title caesar, which Constantine accepted to remove doubts about his legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-25">25 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Milan Maximian gave Constantius the office of Caesar, and the same day Diocletian did the same for <b>Galerius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In ceremonies at Nicomedia and Milan, the two senior emperors stepped down in favour of <b>Galerius</b> and Constantius.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-1">1 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dacia, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.7,26.5&amp;year=292" aria-label="Dacia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Galerius"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4791 6205h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4791 6205h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.7,26.5&amp;year=292">What was here in 292 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
